Plenum-Glee
Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE TRANCE + INDUSTRIAL TECHNO & SCHRANZ + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Lead Vocal
Creative Catalyst: Its first record was cut aboard a generation ship halfway to Proxima Centauri.

Plenum-Glee sounds like an uncontrollable warehouse party erupting inside a falling pressure vessel: pulverizing, distorted 165-BPM industrial schranz kick drums pummel the ribcage while soaring, crystalline progressive trance supersaws and ecstatic, operatic vocal hooks lift listeners into a weightless, tear-streaked frenzy of pure survival joy.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 158–168 BPM; hyper-kinetic, mechanical four-on-the-floor drive powered by red-lined, distorted kicks that hit with hydraulic impact, offset by skittering zero-gravity triplet shakers and syncopated pneumatic valve-chuffs.
* Harmonic Palette: Radiant Lydian and major pentatonic chord progressions that sound impossibly bright, intentionally pitched against detuned, sub-bass square waves to create a manic friction between terror and ecstasy.
* Production Signatures: 
  1. Bulkhead Gate-Reverb: Massive hall reverbs captured in empty metal hulls, violently truncated by ultra-fast noise gates to simulate pressurized airlocks slamming shut.
  2. Schranz-Fold Compression: Extreme analog wavefolding applied to the low-end transients, giving the drums a jagged, splintered punch that cuts clean under melodic elements.
  3. The Hypoxia Build: Cinematic, 64-bar progressive trance sweeps that steadily filter out lower frequencies to simulate oxygen starvation, followed by a concussive drop that explodes into overdriven kicks and joyous, full-throated vocal belting.
* Dynamics: Extreme bi-polar architecture; pulverizing mechanical abuse instantly collapses into vast, cathedral-like vocal breakdowns before slamming back into relentless, jumping euphoria.

Scene & Origin Lore
Plenum-Glee gestated on Deck-14 of the generation vessel SS Almagest at the psychological breaking point of their voyage: Year 44, the exact mathematical coordinate where Sol shrunk to a nondescript speck and Proxima Centauri remained invisible. To combat the crippling nihilism of the "Middle Void," third-generation shipyard mechanics and hydroponics tenders hijacked the auxiliary engine telemetry, routing pneumatic coolant valves and structural test-hammers into vintage software synthesizers. The resulting subculture, known as the Plenum-Jumpers, dressed in thermal flight-suits stripped to the waist, skin painted with phosphorescent coolant grease, wearing oxygen-delivery cannulas as jewelry. Their secret gatherings inside pressurized ballast bays turned into hyperventilating endurance dances—synchronized leaping rituals meant to flex the ship’s titanium floor plates in time with the kicks, celebrating the sheer, defiant miracle of having air in their lungs.

Typical Instruments: Roland JP-8000 supersaws running through abused high-gain mining preamps, industrial scrap-metal idiophones ("The Clatter-Barrow"), modulated coolant release valves, Sherman Filterbanks, and belting bel canto operatic vocals captured on heavy-duty carbon communications mics.

